src/otdnorg.tex:124:The total domatic number of a graph is the maximum number of total dominating sets which partition the vertex set of $G$. In this paper we would like to characterize the cubic graphs with total domatic number at least two.
src/otdnorg.tex:126:%We prove sufficiency of a forbidden subgraph for graphs of total domatic number at least two.
src/otdnorg.tex:159:The \textit{total domatic number} of a graph $G$, denoted by $d_t(G)$, is the maximum number of total dominating sets which partition the vertex set of $G$.
src/otdnorg.tex:163:%Total domination has found applications in network design, game theory, optimization and computational complexity. An example arises in allocating resources to a network \cite{abbas2013deploying}. If each vertex of a graph may only use resources available at the vertex or its neighbors, and if some resource (e.g. a printer) must be available to every node in the network, then copies of that resource must be allocated to a dominating set of the network. If every node in the network can accommodate one resource, then finding the coupon coloring number of the network is equivalent to finding the maximum number of resources that can be made available to every node in the network.
src/otdnorg.tex:172:Theorem~\ref{thm:hypergraph-bipartiteness} immediately reveals that all $r$-regular graphs for $r \geq 4$ can be partitioned into two total dominating sets. It is a well-known fact that a cycle has two disjoint total dominating sets, if and only if its order is divisible by $4$. Knowing that in the Heawood graph, complement of no total dominating set is a total dominating set \cite[p.~51, Exercise~3.14]{jukna2011extremal},
