\begin{abstract}%Let $G$ be a graph.
Let $G$ be a graph.
A total dominating set of $G$ is a set $S$ of vertices of $G$ such that every vertex is adjacent to at least one vertex in $S$.
%A set of vertices $S \subseteq V(G)$ such that every vertex has at least one neighbor in $S$ is called a total dominating set of $G$.
The total domatic number of a graph is the maximum number of total dominating sets which partition the vertex set of $G$. In this paper we would like to characterize the cubic graphs with total domatic number at least two.
%These graphs are of especial interest in graph theory, optimization and network design.
%We prove sufficiency of a forbidden subgraph for graphs of total domatic number at least two.
\end{abstract}

